P(f'Does shading change outcomes? Observational shading, switched on, off or absent, and coarser bands made no change (bit-identical). The coupled variant, in which a hot brain fills its sponge faster, was harmful in a dose-dependent way: {pp(r_fb25.d_sf12, 1)} pp (feedback 0.25), {pp(r_fb5.d_sf12, 1)} pp (0.5) and {ppci(r_fb1)} pp (1.0), with {sg(r_fb1.d_seiz_obs, 2)} seizures per person-year. It was exactly neutral with the sponge off, because shading acts only through the sponge. Shading is thus a significant descriptive feature of the reduced states, the events and the reconciled result, but it only affects outcomes if it is wired into the sponge.')
